body:
A couple notes for posterity on the remaining issues with skew. Before the fix, the T & B transform was just wrong, and as a result the basis was "very non-orthogonal": dot(T, N) and dot(B, N) were not 1 too! This was just an invalid tangent frame. After the fix, dot(T, N) & dot(B, N) should be ~1, but dot(T, B) is not 1 for non-uniform scale because the vectors are transformed independently. This is why the results still diverge vs the pre-scaled mesh: pre-scaled mesh generates orthogonal tangents from the pre-transformed data.

This can be fixed by ensuring orthogonality, which would align the top & bottom images more closely (once you place the camera better so that the specular highlights are the same...). In fact, if you look at what http://www.mikktspace.com *actually* recommends (see "Pixel Shader Transformation"), we do not follow this recommendation exactly because we reconstruct in the *vertex* shader, and in *object* space:

> In regards to the interpolated tangent space the baker in both Blender and the xnormal plugin will keep the vertex normal and the vertex tangent normalized in the vertex shader. However, in the pixel shader the "unnormalized" and interpolated vertex normal and tangent are used to decode the tangent space normal. The bitangent is constructed here to avoid the use of an additional interpolater and again is NOT normalized.

> // vNt is the tangent space normal
> vB = sign * cross(vN, vT);
> vNout = normalize( vNt.x * vT + vNt.y * vB + vNt.z * vN );

Their recommendation to reconstruct in the fragment shader is efficient and is most commonly used in the industry vs the current Godot approach. However, this runs into the problem that Godot seems to allow users to use BINORMAL in their custom vertex fragments; moving bitangent reconstruction to fragment shader unconditionally would break this, and maintaining both paths with different behavior feels wrong.

I also attempted to simply fix this in the vertex shader, by moving the bitangent reconstruction after the transform. This works fine with the user shaders, but I was hoping it would be cheaper (avoiding a matrix mul) - unfortunately, we need to correct the sign according to whether the model matrix flips the mesh (negative scale), which then requires code like this to run instead of the `binormal` transform:

```glsl
binormal_sign *= determinant(mat3(modelview));
binormal = normalize(cross(normal, tangent) * binormal_sign));
```

This is not *bad*: we are moving `normalize(cross)` to a later place in the shader, and replacing `mat3 * vec3` with `determinant`, which should be the same number of FLOPS, plus a scalar mul. But I was hoping it would be cheaper and it's not, and probably fixing the remaining skew is not super critical in practice so I left this out of this PR but I welcome future improvements in this area :)
